### Bad Code Snippet: Vulnerable to SQL Injection

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class BadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a connection to the database
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "username", "password");

        // Create a statement object
        Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();

        // Construct a SQL query with user input
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + username + "'";

        // Execute the query
        ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);

        // Print the results
        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println(rs.getString(1));
        }

        // Close the resources
        rs.close();
        stmt.close();
        conn.close();
    }

    // Note: This code is vulnerable to SQL injection attacks
    // because it directly inserts user input into the SQL query.
    public static String username = "John Doe";
}
```

***


### Good Code Snippet: Secure with Prepared Statements

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class GoodExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a connection to the database
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "username", "password");

        // Create a prepared statement object
        PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name =?");

        // Set the user input as a parameter
        pstmt.setString(1, username);

        // Execute the query
        ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();

        // Print the results
        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println(rs.getString(1));
        }

        // Close the resources
        rs.close();
        pstmt.close();
        conn.close();
    }

    public static String username = "John Doe";
}
```

In the good code snippet, we use a prepared statement to separate the SQL query from the user input. This prevents SQL injection attacks by ensuring that the user input is treated as a parameter, not part of the SQL code.

